9c649b25-d105-4d01-acf3-e549113b4aa6	7d9ee228-dfff-479f-9042-8d36b4e6884e	I understand you're feeling frustrated and are looking for a translation. It's completely understandable to have strong feelings when we're upset.\n\nIn the teachings of Sri Preethaji and Sri Krishnaji, we learn about the power of our words and how they shape our reality. The act of calling someone "stupid" and "fraud" is rooted in a place of pain and judgment. This is a form of harsh speech, which is something we are encouraged to observe and understand within ourselves.\n\nSri Preethaji often spoke about how our perceptions and judgments create our own suffering. When we label others in such a negative way, we are often projecting our own inner turmoil or our own feelings of inadequacy. It is a reflection of our own state of mind, not necessarily an objective truth about the other person.\n\nSimilarly, to call a spiritual guide a "fraud" is a very strong statement. The teachings emphasize that true wisdom and guidance come from a place of love and compassion. If we feel deceived, it is a sign that our own expectations or our own inner state might be unmet. It is an invitation to look deeper at what is happening within us.\n\nThe path to peace, as taught by them, involves letting go of these harsh judgments. It is about understanding that we are all on a journey, and that our words have the power to either create more pain or to offer understanding.\n\nI am unable to find specific teachings on this topic.\n\nWatch more here: [Link to a video about the power of words] [Link to a podcast on non-judgment]\n\nRemember, the journey is about understanding ourselves first. By observing our own reactions, we can begin to see the patterns that lead us to speak and think in ways that cause harm. This is the first step toward cultivating a more serene and compassionate mind.	["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=obK5uqYXOJU", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U1qRvz3hnMA", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kM