dde2fb76-53d6-47b0-97c5-a9db4e7046b0	6b5b643e-d410-43f1-89ea-653794401953	My dear friend, I'm glad you asked about the story of the fish seller and the flower seller. This story is a beautiful illustration of the principles of the Beautiful State, which Sri Preethaji and Sri Krishnaji have shared with us. The moral of this story is that our perception of reality is not the reality itself. The fish seller and the flower seller are two individuals who see the same reality, but their perceptions of it are vastly different. The fish seller sees the world as a place of scarcity, where there is never enough, and the flower seller sees the world as a place of abundance, where there is always enough. This story teaches us that our perception of reality is shaped by our thoughts, emotions, and experiences. It reminds us that we have the power to choose how we see the world, and that our perception can either limit or liberate us. In the context of the Beautiful State, this story encourages us to let go of our limited perceptions and to see the world as a place of abundance and possibility. It invites us to cultivate a mindset of gratitude, appreciation, and wonder, and to see the beauty and wonder in every moment. As Sri Preethaji and Sri Krishnaji have said, "The Beautiful State is not a place, it's a state of being. It's a state of being that is characterized by love, compassion, and awareness." This story is a beautiful reminder of the importance of cultivating this state of being in our daily lives.	["Beautiful State — Sri Preethaji (YouTube https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TqxxCYnAxo8)", "Beautiful State — Sri Preethaji (YouTube https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TqxxCYnAxo8)"]	1.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	f		8.0	2026-07-06 13:28:59+00	{"intent": "FACTUAL", "model_used": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "query_tier": "tier2_simple", "memory_used": true, "citation_urls": ["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fbd7ln2i9dw"], "model_provider": "nim", "relevant_count": 1, "reranked_count": 1, "rout