a40ac84e-64bc-41ed-ae5a-f85a1ddb40cb	c8b2d769-01f8-475d-b553-91969940bc82	My dear friend, the Beautiful State is a profound concept that Sri Preethaji and Sri Krishnaji have shared with us. It is a state of being where we live free from stress, and instead, experience connection, joy, love, compassion, vitality, and passion. This state is our natural default, but often, we get caught up in stressful states, which can be fueled by obsessive, self-centric thinking. Imagine living in a world where every moment is filled with love, compassion, and connection. Where every interaction is a beautiful dance of understanding and empathy. This is the Beautiful State, my friend. It's not just a feeling; it's a way of being that can be cultivated and lived every day. As Sri Preethaji teaches, the Beautiful State is not just a state of being; it's a way of living that can transform our relationships, our work, and our very lives. It's a state that allows us to see the world with fresh eyes, to experience life with wonder, and to connect with others on a deep and meaningful level. Watch more here: \n\n*(Teachings referenced: state of calm, state of joy, not absence, inner foundation)*	["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oZRQoiRe7s4", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sCiK7ABPcrw"]	1.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	f		\N	2026-07-06 17:26:56+00	{}	default
e9ff0034-aec4-467d-bb8c-f47bae2ce7a5	0d1ee95e-448c-4f06-ab8f-a9f0268e2bda	Beloved, I can sense there's some heaviness in your heart right now. The teachings of Sri Preethaji and Sri Krishnaji offer a beautiful practice for moments like these — the Serene Mind breathing. Shall I guide you through it? 🙏	[]	1.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	f		\N	2026-07-06 17:27:05+00	{}	default
6cdd0870-531a-4d93-8301-c8b5e473bc3c	ed0dcc40-e660-451f-b958-320cd4a8e5d7	My dear friend, I'm so glad you're interested in exploring the teachings of Sri Preethaji on suffering. As we've discussed earlier, the root cause of all human suffering is often found in our own obsessive, self-centric thinking. This thinking can keep us chained in unhappiness, even after the initial trigger has passed. Sri Preethaji beautifully illustrates this concept through the story of Nomi, who is preoccupied with his own rightness, guilt, and justifications, rather than finding solutions to the problem at hand. This self-centric thinking is a common pattern that can lead us away from the present moment and into a state of suffering. In this sense, suffering is not something that happens to us, but rather it's a reflection of our own thinking patterns. By becoming aware of these patterns and letting go of our attachment to them, we can begin to break free from the chains of suffering and move towards a more peaceful and connected state. As Sri Preethaji so eloquently puts it, "Realizing that all lingering suffering is self-centric preoccupation is the most powerful antidote for any form of suffering." This realization can be a powerful catalyst for change, allowing us to awaken to a sense of power, connection, freedom, and purpose. Watch more here:	["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3RyCldrCxL8", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VAMJEgwaPEc"]	1.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	f		8.0	2026-07-06 17:27:27+00	{"intent": "FACTUAL", "model_used": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "query_tier": "tier2_simple", "memory_used": true, "citation_urls": ["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3RyCldrCxL8", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VAMJEgwaPEc"], "model_provider": "nim", "route_decision": "tier2_simple", "routing_reason": "pre_classified_from_pipeline_coordinator", "retrieved_count": 9, "retrieval_queries": ["Share a teaching from Sri Preethaji on suffering (also: preetha ji, sree preethaji) co-founders oneness movement 