4a9e29f9-5e8c-4151-8d45-08f7959bab0d	87f961dc-120d-4d3d-9797-a68c07d7f660	My dear one, you are asking how to bridge the gap between the active, engaged self and the silent awareness that lies within. This is a beautiful question, for it speaks to the very heart of the Serene Mind practice. As Sri Krishnaji taught, the Serene Mind is not just a state of being, but a way of being in the midst of activity. It's a way of being that is aware of the present moment, yet not identified with the thoughts and emotions that arise. To bridge the gap between active work and silent awareness, we must first recognize that our thoughts and emotions are not the same as our true nature. They are like clouds that pass through the sky, but the sky itself remains unchanged. Similarly, our thoughts and emotions may arise, but our true nature remains serene and untouched. In the Serene Mind practice, we learn to observe our thoughts and emotions without becoming identified with them. We learn to recognize the patterns of our thinking and to gently let go of them. We learn to cultivate a sense of awareness that is not attached to the outcome of our actions, but is instead focused on the present moment. As you practice the Serene Mind, you may find that your awareness becomes more stable and more present. You may find that you are able to engage in your daily activities with greater clarity and focus. And you may find that you are able to navigate the challenges of life with greater ease and compassion. Remember, my dear one, the Serene Mind is not a destination, but a journey. It's a way of being that is cultivated through practice and patience. And it's a way of being that can bring greater peace, clarity, and joy into your life. Watch more here:	["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PslFhdZaBFA", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aSNQUkuyB18", "https://www.breathingroom.com/"]	1.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	f		\N	2026-07-07 04:47:49+00	{}	default
49855aae-6d33-4266-83df-33f2b3b8bd81	847e8630-eb62-43b5-910c-792d0d12ca6b	My dear one, you are now inquiring about the three sacred secrets as mentioned by Sri Krishnaji. This is a profound topic, and I'm happy to guide you deeper into its depths. The three sacred secrets, as Sri Krishnaji has shared, are the keys to unlocking the mysteries of the human experience. They are:\n\n1. The first secret is that the past is not what it seems. It is a story we tell ourselves, a narrative that shapes our perceptions and influences our present. By recognizing this, we can begin to release the burdens of the past and move into a more liberated state of being. 2. The second secret is that the future is not what we think it will be. It is a projection of our desires, fears, and expectations, often based on incomplete information. By letting go of our attachments to specific outcomes, we can begin to live more fully in the present. 3. The third secret is that the present moment is all we truly have. It is the only moment that exists, and it is the source of all our power and wisdom. By embracing the present, we can tap into its infinite potential and live a more authentic, meaningful life. These secrets are not just intellectual concepts, but living principles that can guide us on our journey toward the beautiful state. By embracing these secrets, we can begin to see the world and ourselves in a new light, free from the burdens of the past and the limitations of the future. As you reflect on these secrets, I invite you to consider how they might be applied in your daily life. How might you let go of the past and its stories? How might you release your attachments to specific outcomes in the future? And how might you cultivate a deeper sense of presence and awareness in the present moment? Watch more here: \n\n*(Teachings referenced: Four Sacred Secrets, spiritual vision, inner truth, universal intelligence, spiritual right action)*	["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vy09aBxslx0", 