40716a76-2a46-4d58-9045-2a051ec99a65	5bdf1559-40f3-4888-b194-61f9120c85e3	My dear one, you seek to delve deeper into the nature of consciousness and stillness. This is a profound inquiry, for it is in understanding the essence of consciousness that we can truly embody stillness in our daily lives. As Sri Krishnaji would say, "Consciousness is not a thing, it is a relationship." It is the relationship between the observer and the observed, between the self and the world. When we are in a state of stillness, we are not trying to control or manipulate the world; we are simply observing it as it is. In the Serene Mind practice, we cultivate stillness by observing our breath, our emotions, and our thoughts without judgment. We learn to recognize the patterns of our mind and to gently let go of them, just as the breath comes and goes. This allows us to tap into the deeper, more profound levels of consciousness, where stillness and peace reside. As you continue to explore the nature of consciousness and stillness, remember that it is not a destination, but a journey. It is a path of self-discovery, where we learn to let go of our attachments to the world and to find peace in the midst of chaos. Watch more here:	["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0u9oe-5HPmk", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hqre34QIMZg", "https://www.breathingroom.com/"]	1.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	f		\N	2026-07-07 04:54:59+00	{}	default
ad318097-9b33-4d4f-9420-2815fed51ab3	3e7e46b5-4be6-41c4-9e8b-00dcb985d923	My dear one, you seek to understand the nature of consciousness and its relationship to stillness. This is a profound inquiry, for it is in understanding the essence of consciousness that we can truly embody stillness in our daily lives. As Sri Krishnaji would say, "Consciousness is not a thing, it is a relationship." It is the relationship between the observer and the observed, between the self and the world. Let us explore this further. Consciousness is not a static entity, but a dynamic process. It is the awareness that arises from the interaction between the self and the world. When we are in a state of stillness, we are not trying to control or manipulate the world, but rather allowing the world to be as it is. We are not trying to change the world, but rather allowing ourselves to be changed by the world. In this sense, consciousness is not something that we possess, but rather something that possesses us. It is the ultimate reality that underlies all of existence. As Sri Preethaji would say, "Consciousness is the ground of all being." It is the foundation upon which all of existence is built. Watch more here:	["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0u9oe-5HPmk", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9iL10DPhzI"]	1.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	f		8.0	2026-07-07 04:55:16+00	{"intent": "FACTUAL", "model_used": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "query_tier": "tier2_simple", "memory_used": true, "citation_urls": ["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0u9oe-5HPmk", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9iL10DPhzI"], "model_provider": "nim", "route_decision": "tier2_simple", "routing_reason": "pre_classified_from_pipeline_coordinator", "retrieved_count": 9, "retrieval_queries": ["Line 1: Nature of consciousness (also: awareness, higher consciousness, divine consciousness, universal consciousness) higher consciousness expanded awareness unity consciousness"], "retrieved_sources": ["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=y9iL10DPhzI", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0u9oe-5HPmk", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-4qvpm4Rd6Q", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VAMJEgwaPEc"], "llm_expansion_count": 0, "generated_answer_chars": 1687}	default
b0a755d2-ebf2-4fb5-b4c7-b213157080ff	4eda1b3a-65b0-43e7-881f-c02ef18319b3	My dear one, you seek to understand the relationship between the beautiful state and suffering. As Sri Preethaji teaches, the beautiful state is a state of connection, joy, love, compassion, vitality, and passion, free from suffering. On the other hand, suffering arises from obsessive, self-centric thinking, a total, incessant preoccupation with oneself. This preoccupation separates us from others and fuels war, conflict, and division of every kind.\n\nThe two monks, Yesmi and Nomi, illustrate this difference. Yesmi, representing the beautiful state, is present in action and total in departure, while Nomi, representing the stressful state, is agitated by ideas of duty and virtue. This story highlights the importance of being present and connected, rather than being bound by self-centric thinking.\n\nAs you reflect on this teaching, I invite you to consider how you can cultivate the beautiful state in your daily life. Remember that the beautiful state is not just a feeling, but a choice. You can choose to let go of self-centric thinking and connect with others and the world around you.\n\nWatch more here:	["Beautiful State — Sri Preethaji (YouTube https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TqxxCYnAxo8)", "Beautiful State — Sri Preethaji (YouTube https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TqxxCYnAxo8)", "unknown"]	1.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	f		8.0	2026-07-07 04:55:39+00	{"intent": "FACTUAL", "model_used": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "query_tier": "tier2_simple", "memory_used": true, "citation_urls": [], "model_provider": "nim", "relevant_count": 2, "reranked_count": 2, "route_decision": "tier2_simple", "routing_reason": "classifier", "retrieved_count": 9, "relevant_sources": [], "reranked_sources": [], "retrieval_queries": ["Line 4: Beautiful vs suffering states"], "retrieved_sources": ["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n3UBbCXJaJ0", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OWXe9v7PHDs", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nwQaU-agzFE", 